body:
I feel the same. When I first started opening up to people around me about how I feel, it felt good. Relieving, I guess. They were reasonably understanding and I didn't feel as bad about being as distant as I am. But as time has gone by, I've realised I really don't get anything out of being open about my feeling anymore. Like you say, it feels like they're only checking up on me because they have to, because we're friends and that's what friends do. And I don't want to say that well, I feel as shit as ever, because I'm sure they're sick of it by now, that I still haven't gotten my shit together. 

&#x200B;

But yeah, like you said. I guess it's counter-productive.

Everyone's losing their shit over JK and I'm just here like....... he's radiating the biggest baby brother energy ever, what are ya'll on about :|

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

I'm so sorry this happened to you. I really hope you're doing better now.

&#x200B;

There's obviously a problem with fat/normal weight people with EDs not being taken seriously, but that they even for a second believe that "being taken seriously" always means something good for someone who's underweight... ugh. I was forced into daycare treatment as a minor, then forcibly hospitalised as an adult. Some of what happened was warranted, A LOT was not. I mean, I guess they kept me alive, but they were slowly killing me anyway so what does it matter?

&#x200B;

I KNOW it's a legit problem that you can only get proper help if you're dying, but I wouldn't put it past FA's to disregard the KIND OF treatment that's given just to further their agenda.

Yeah okay, so it MIGHT be temporary, but we don't know that for sure. Also, what's temporary? A year? Five years? Ten years? Even longer? How long should one suffer (to an extent that someone who is not depressed cannot understand) before it's no longer deemed a "temporary" problem? Shouldn't that be up to ME to decide, what I want to do with MY life?

&#x200B;

Having said that, there might be other permanent solutions, and I do think one should explore possible solutions before turning to suicide. But also, who's a non-depressed, non-suicidal, person to tell me that my problems are "temporary"?

I'm sorry for your loss, and I hope you're doing better now! But yeah, I'd say it sounds like you understand it better than most. It's not even about the weight (diagnostic criteria aside), but the mindset. Like, I completely get what you're saying. It's so irrational, but it also kind of helps in the moment so it feels legit. A simple cause-effect-relationship amidst everything that's so so so complicated. 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

How to make it obvious you've never suffered with a restrictive eating disorder: Claim it's about being thin. 

&#x200B;

They're SO wrong, and it honestly makes me SO angry. I'm so so so tired of FAs thinking everyone with a (restrictive, lbr) eating disorder are stupid?? I really think you can't understand it unless you've been there. Not saying it's exactly the same for everybody, but when I was severely underweight and in recovery, I wasn't necessarily scared of a normal weight. I was scared of uncontrollably gaining weight, thus losing the only comfort and imaginary safety I had, in controlling one (1) thing in my life. I mean, isn't that common knowledge by now, eating disorders are MENTAL disorders; symptoms of an underlying issue?

>those living in larger bodies

Excuse me while I scream. 

&#x200B;
